/* Generated by tools/build_lines.py from picks/g13.txt. Every line is from: NCERT XI Part 2 (old). Do not edit by hand. */
(function () {
  const R = window.RES;
  R.addLines("g13", "NCERT XI Part 2 (old)", [{"sec": "p-block: general","ids": [760],"t": "p-block: last electron enters the outermost p orbital (max 6 p electrons).","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 56,"o": "In p-block elements the last electron enters the outermost p orbital. As we know that the number of p orbitals is three and, therefore, the maximum number of electrons that can be accommodated in a set of p orbitals is six."},{"sec": "p-block: general","ids": [762,763],"t": "Groups headed by B, C, N, O, F, He; valence configuration ns^2np^1-6 (except He).","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 56,"o": "Boron, carbon, nitrogen, oxygen, fluorine and helium head the groups. Their valence shell electronic configuration is ns^2np^1-6 (except for He)."},{"sec": "p-block: general","ids": [767,768],"t": "Max oxidation state = total valence electrons (s + p); number of possible oxidation states increases to the right.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 56,"o": "The maximum oxidation state shown by a p-block element is equal to the total number of valence electrons (i.e., the sum of the s- and p-electrons). Clearly, the number of possible oxidation states increases towards the right of the periodic table."},{"sec": "p-block: general","ids": [769],"t": "Other oxidation states usually differ from the group oxidation state by 2.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 56,"o": "In addition to this so called group oxidation state, p-block elements may show other oxidation states which normally, but not necessarily, differ from the total number of valence electrons by unit of two."},{"sec": "p-block: general","ids": [771,772,773],"t": "B, C, N families: group oxidation state is most stable for lighter members; **(group – 2) becomes progressively more stable for heavier members (inert pair effect)**.","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 56,"o": "In boron, carbon and nitrogen families the group oxidation state is the most stable state for the lighter elements in the group. However, the oxidation state two unit less than the group oxidation state becomes progressively more stable for the heavier elements in each group. The occurrences of oxidation states two unit less than the group oxidation states are sometime attributed to the 'inert pair effect'."},{"sec": "p-block: general","ids": [774],"t": "Table 11.1: Group 13 ns^2np^1, group oxidation state +3, other +1; Group 14 ns^2np^2, +4, other +2, -4; Group 15 ns^2np^3, +5, other +3, -3; Group 16 ns^2np^4, +6, other +4, +2, -2; Group 17 ns^2np^5, +7, other +5, +3, +1, -1; Group 18 ns^2np^6 (1s^2 for He), +8, other +6, +4, +2.","exc": false,"tab": true,"src": "old11","page": 57,"o": "Table 11.1: Group 13 ns^2np^1, group oxidation state +3, other +1; Group 14 ns^2np^2, +4, other +2, -4; Group 15 ns^2np^3, +5, other +3, -3; Group 16 ns^2np^4, +6, other +4, +2, -2; Group 17 ns^2np^5, +7, other +5, +3, +1, -1; Group 18 ns^2np^6 (1s^2 for He), +8, other +6, +4, +2."},{"sec": "p-block: general","ids": [775,776,777],"t": "Non-metals and metalloids exist only in the p-block; non-metallic character falls down a group; the heaviest member is the most metallic.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 57,"o": "It is interesting to note that the non-metals and metalloids exist only in the p-block of the periodic table. The non-metallic character of elements decreases down the group. In fact the heaviest element in each p-block group is the most metallic in nature."},{"sec": "p-block: general","ids": [779,780],"t": "Non-metals: higher ∆iH and EN than metals, so they form anions; metals form cations.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 57,"o": "In general, non-metals have higher ionisation enthalpies and higher electronegativities than the metals. Hence, in contrast to metals which readily form cations, non-metals readily form anions."},{"sec": "p-block: general","ids": [781,782],"t": "Reactive metal + reactive non-metal: ionic (large ∆EN). Non-metal + non-metal: covalent (small ∆EN).","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 57,"o": "The compounds formed by highly reactive non-metals with highly reactive metals are generally ionic because of large differences in their electronegativities. On the other hand, compounds formed between non-metals themselves are largely covalent in character because of small differences in their electronegativities."},{"sec": "p-block: general","ids": [783,784],"t": "Oxides show the trend: non-metal oxides acidic or neutral, metal oxides basic.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 57,"o": "The change of non-metallic to metallic character can be best illustrated by the nature of oxides they form. The non-metal oxides are acidic or neutral whereas metal oxides are basic in nature."},{"sec": "p-block: general","ids": [785,786,787],"t": "First member differs (1) in size and size-dependent properties, like Li and Be in the s-block.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 57,"o": "The first member of p-block differs from the remaining members of their corresponding group in two major respects. First is the size and all other properties which depend on size. Thus, the lightest p-block elements show the same kind of differences as the lightest s-block elements, lithium and beryllium."},{"sec": "p-block: general","ids": [788],"t": "(2) Heavier members (period 3 on) have d orbitals in the valence shell; period 2 does not.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 57,"o": "The second important difference, which applies only to the p-block elements, arises from the effect of d-orbitals in the valence shell of heavier elements (starting from the third period onwards) and their lack in second period elements."},{"sec": "p-block: general","ids": [789,790,791],"t": "Period 2 p-elements (from B): **max covalence 4** (2s + three 2p). Period 3 has vacant 3d (between 3p and 4s) and can exceed 4.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 57,"o": "The second period elements of p-groups starting from boron are restricted to a maximum covalence of four (using 2s and three 2p orbitals). In contrast, the third period elements of p-groups with the electronic configuration 3s^2 3p^n have the vacant 3d orbitals lying between the 3p and the 4s levels of energy. Using these d-orbitals the third period elements can expand their covalence above four."},{"sec": "p-block: general","ids": [792],"t": "**B forms only [BF4]^-; Al forms [AlF6]^3-.**","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 57,"o": "For example, while boron forms only [BF4]^-, aluminium gives [AlF6]^3- ion."},{"sec": "p-block: general","ids": [793,794],"t": "Size plus d-orbital availability controls π-bond formation.","exc": false,"tab": true,"src": "old11","page": 57,"o": "The presence of these d-orbitals influences the chemistry of the heavier elements in a number of other ways. The combined effect of size and availability of d orbitals considerably influences the ability of these elements to form π bonds."},{"sec": "p-block: general","ids": [795,796],"t": "First member forms pπ–pπ multiple bonds to itself (C=C, C≡C, N≡N) and to other period-2 atoms (C=O, C=N, C≡N, N=O).","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 57,"o": "The first member of a group differs from the heavier members in its ability to form pπ - pπ multiple bonds to itself (e.g., C=C, C≡C, N≡N) and to other second row elements (e.g., C=O, C=N, C≡N, N=O)."},{"sec": "p-block: general","ids": [797,798,799],"t": "Heavier elements form weaker π bonds using d orbitals (dπ–pπ or dπ–dπ); higher-energy d orbitals add less stability than pπ–pπ.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 58,"o": "This type of π-bonding is not particularly strong for the heavier p-block elements. The heavier elements do form π bonds but this involves d orbitals (dπ - pπ or dπ - dπ). As the d orbitals are of higher energy than the p orbitals, they contribute less to the overall stability of molecules than does pπ-pπ bonding of the second row elements."},{"sec": "p-block: general","ids": [800,801],"t": "Heavier members can have higher coordination number in the same oxidation state: NO3^- (3-coordinate, pπ–pπ) vs PO4^3- (4-coordinate, s, p, d contribute to π).","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 58,"o": "However, the coordination number in species of heavier elements may be higher than for the first element in the same oxidation state. For example, in +5 oxidation state both N and P form oxoanions: NO3^- (three-coordination with π-bond involving one nitrogen p-orbital) and PO4^3- (four-coordination involving s, p and d orbitals contributing to the π-bond)."},{"sec": "Group 13: occurrence","ids": [802,803],"t": "B: typical non-metal; Al: metal with many B-like properties; Ga, In, Tl: almost exclusively metallic.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 58,"o": "This group elements show a wide variation in properties. Boron is a typical non-metal, aluminium is a metal but shows many chemical similarities to boron, and gallium, indium and thallium are almost exclusively metallic in character."},{"sec": "Group 13: occurrence","ids": [804],"t": "B ores: orthoboric acid H3BO3, borax Na2B4O7·10H2O, kernite Na2B4O7·4H2O.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 58,"o": "Boron is a fairly rare element, mainly occurs as orthoboric acid, (H3BO3), borax, Na2B4O7·10H2O, and kernite, Na2B4O7·4H2O."},{"sec": "Group 13: occurrence","ids": [807],"t": "B isotopes: ^10B (19%), ^11B (81%).","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 58,"o": "There are two isotopic forms of boron ^10B (19%) and ^11B (81%)."},{"sec": "Group 13: occurrence","ids": [808],"t": "Al: most abundant metal, third most abundant element in the crust (8.3%) after O (45.5%) and Si (27.7%).","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 58,"o": "Aluminium is the most abundant metal and the third most abundant element in the earth's crust (8.3% by mass) after oxygen (45.5%) and Si (27.7%)."},{"sec": "Group 13: occurrence","ids": [809,810],"t": "Al minerals: bauxite Al2O3·2H2O, cryolite Na3AlF6.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 58,"o": "Bauxite, Al2O3·2H2O and cryolite, Na3AlF6 are the important minerals of aluminium. In India it is found as mica in Madhya Pradesh, Karnataka, Orissa and Jammu."},{"sec": "Group 13: atomic properties","ids": [812,813],"t": "ns^2np^1. B, Al: noble-gas core; Ga, In: + 10 d electrons; Tl: + 14 f + 10 d.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 58,"o": "The outer electronic configuration of these elements is ns^2np^1. A close look at the electronic configuration suggests that while boron and aluminium have noble gas core, gallium and indium have noble gas plus 10 d-electrons, and thallium has noble gas plus 14 f electrons plus 10 d-electrons cores."},{"sec": "Group 13: atomic properties","ids": [815,816,817],"t": "Radius should rise down the group, **but r(Ga) < r(Al)**.","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 58,"o": "On moving down the group, for each successive member one extra shell of electrons is added and, therefore, atomic radius is expected to increase. However, a deviation can be seen. Atomic radius of Ga is less than that of Al."},{"sec": "Group 13: atomic properties","ids": [818,819],"t": "Ga's 10 d electrons shield poorly, so Ga (135 pm) < Al (143 pm).","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 58,"o": "The presence of additional 10 d-electrons offer only poor screening effect for the outer electrons from the increased nuclear charge in gallium. Consequently, the atomic radius of gallium (135 pm) is less than that of aluminium (143 pm)."},{"sec": "Group 13: atomic properties","ids": [819,820,821],"t": "∆iH does not fall smoothly: B → Al decreases (size), but **jumps at Al → Ga and In → Tl** (poor d and f shielding).","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 58,"o": "The ionisation enthalpy values as expected from the general trends do not decrease smoothly down the group. The decrease from B to Al is associated with increase in size. The observed discontinuity in the ionisation enthalpy values between Al and Ga, and between In and Tl are due to inability of d- and f-electrons, which have low screening effect, to compensate the increase in nuclear charge."},{"sec": "Group 13: atomic properties","ids": [822,823],"t": "∆iH1 < ∆iH2 < ∆iH3; the sum of the first three is very high.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 58,"o": "The order of ionisation enthalpies, as expected, is ∆iH1 < ∆iH2 < ∆iH3. The sum of the first three ionisation enthalpies for each of the elements is very high."},{"sec": "Group 13: atomic properties","ids": [824,825,826],"t": "**EN falls B → Al, then rises slightly** (irregular atomic sizes).","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 58,"o": "Down the group, electronegativity first decreases from B to Al and then increases marginally. This is because of the discrepancies in atomic size of the elements."},{"sec": "Group 13: atomic properties","ids": [846],"t": "Table 11.2: ∆iH1/kJ mol^-1 B 801, Al 577, Ga 579, In 558, Tl 589. Electronegativity 2.0, 1.5, 1.6, 1.7, 1.8. Melting point/K 2453, 933, 303, 430, 576. Boiling point/K 3923, 2740, 2676, 2353, 1730. E°(M^3+/M)/V Al -1.66, Ga -0.56, In -0.34, Tl +1.26. Density/g cm^-3 2.35, 2.70, 5.90, 7.31, 11.85.","exc": false,"tab": true,"src": "old11","page": 59,"o": "Table 11.2: ∆iH1/kJ mol^-1 B 801, Al 577, Ga 579, In 558, Tl 589. Electronegativity 2.0, 1.5, 1.6, 1.7, 1.8. Melting point/K 2453, 933, 303, 430, 576. Boiling point/K 3923, 2740, 2676, 2353, 1730. E°(M^3+/M)/V Al -1.66, Ga -0.56, In -0.34, Tl +1.26. Density/g cm^-3 2.35, 2.70, 5.90, 7.31, 11.85."},{"sec": "Group 13: physical properties","ids": [827,828,829,830],"t": "B: non-metal, extremely hard, black; many allotropes; unusually high m.p. (strong lattice).","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 59,"o": "Boron is non-metallic in nature. It is extremely hard and black coloured solid. It exists in many allotropic forms. Due to very strong crystalline lattice, boron has unusually high melting point."},{"sec": "Group 13: physical properties","ids": [831],"t": "Others: soft metals, low m.p., high conductivity.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 59,"o": "Rest of the members are soft metals with low melting point and high electrical conductivity."},{"sec": "Group 13: physical properties","ids": [832,833],"t": "**Ga: m.p. 303 K (liquid in summer), b.p. 2676 K**, used to measure high temperatures.","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 59,"o": "It is worthwhile to note that gallium with unusually low melting point (303K), could exist in liquid state during summer. Its high boiling point (2676K) makes it a useful material for measuring high temperatures."},{"sec": "Group 13: physical properties","ids": [834],"t": "Density increases B → Tl.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 59,"o": "Density of the elements increases down the group from boron to thallium."},{"sec": "Group 13: oxidation states and chemical properties","ids": [835,836],"t": "**B: very high sum of first three ∆iH (small size), so no B^3+; only covalent compounds.**","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 59,"o": "Due to small size of boron, the sum of its first three ionization enthalpies is very high. This prevents it to form +3 ions and forces it to form only covalent compounds."},{"sec": "Group 13: oxidation states and chemical properties","ids": [837,838],"t": "Al: lower sum, forms Al^3+; highly electropositive.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 59,"o": "But as we move from B to Al, the sum of the first three ionisation enthalpies of Al considerably decreases, and is therefore able to form Al^3+ ions. In fact, aluminium is a highly electropositive metal."},{"sec": "Group 13: oxidation states and chemical properties","ids": [838,839],"t": "Down the group poor d/f shielding holds the ns^2 pair tightly (inert pair effect), so often only the p electron bonds.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 59,"o": "However, down the group, due to poor shielding effect of intervening d and f orbitals, the increased effective nuclear charge holds ns electrons tightly (responsible for inert pair effect) and thereby, restricting their participation in bonding. As a result of this, only p-orbital electron may be involved in bonding."},{"sec": "Group 13: oxidation states and chemical properties","ids": [840,841,842],"t": "Ga, In, Tl show +1 and +3; **+1 stability: Al < Ga < In < Tl. In Tl, +1 is predominant and +3 is strongly oxidising.**","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 59,"o": "In fact in Ga, In and Tl, both +1 and +3 oxidation states are observed. The relative stability of +1 oxidation state progressively increases for heavier elements: Al<Ga<In<Tl. In thallium +1 oxidation state is predominant whereas the +3 oxidation state is highly oxidising in character."},{"sec": "Group 13: oxidation states and chemical properties","ids": [843],"t": "+1 compounds are more ionic than +3 compounds.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 59,"o": "The compounds in +1 oxidation state, as expected from energy considerations, are more ionic than those in +3 oxidation state."},{"sec": "Group 13: oxidation states and chemical properties","ids": [844,847,848],"t": "+3 compounds (e.g. BF3) have only 6 electrons on the central atom: electron deficient, Lewis acids.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 59,"o": "In trivalent state, the number of electrons around the central atom in a molecule of the compounds of these elements (e.g., boron in BF3) will be only six. Such electron deficient molecules have tendency to accept a pair of electrons to achieve stable electronic configuration and thus, behave as Lewis acids."},{"sec": "Group 13: oxidation states and chemical properties","ids": [849,850],"t": "Lewis acidity decreases down the group. BCl3 + NH3 → BCl3·NH3","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 60,"o": "The tendency to behave as Lewis acid decreases with the increase in the size down the group. BCl3 easily accepts a lone pair of electrons from ammonia to form BCl3·NH3."},{"sec": "Group 13: oxidation states and chemical properties","ids": [851],"t": "AlCl3 attains stability by dimerising.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 60,"o": "AlCl3 achieves stability by forming a dimer."},{"sec": "Group 13: oxidation states and chemical properties","ids": [851,852],"t": "Covalent +3 compounds hydrolyse; trichlorides give tetrahedral [M(OH)4]^- (M sp^3).","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 60,"o": "In trivalent state most of the compounds being covalent are hydrolysed in water. For example, the trichlorides on hydrolysis in water form tetrahedral [M(OH)4]^- species; the hybridisation state of element M is sp^3."},{"sec": "Group 13: oxidation states and chemical properties","ids": [853,854],"t": "AlCl3 in acidified water: octahedral [Al(H2O)6]^3+ (3d involved, sp^3d^2).","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 60,"o": "Aluminium chloride in acidified aqueous solution forms octahedral [Al(H2O)6]^3+ ion. In this complex ion, the 3d orbitals of Al are involved and the hybridisation state of Al is sp^3d^2."},{"sec": "Group 13: oxidation states and chemical properties","ids": [855,856,857],"t": "E°: Al^3+/Al -1.66 V, Tl^3+/Tl +1.26 V. **Al^3+ forms readily; Tl^3+ is unstable and strongly oxidising, so Tl^+ is more stable in solution.**","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 60,"o": "E° for Al^3+/Al is -1.66 V and that of Tl^3+/Tl is +1.26 V. Aluminium has high tendency to make Al^3+(aq) ions, whereas Tl^3+ is not only unstable in solution but is a powerful oxidising agent also. Thus Tl^+ is more stable in solution than Tl^3+."},{"sec": "Group 13: oxidation states and chemical properties","ids": [858],"t": "Al is more electropositive than Tl.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 60,"o": "Aluminium being able to form +3 ions easily, is more electropositive than thallium."},{"sec": "Group 13: oxidation states and chemical properties","ids": [859,860],"t": "Crystalline B is unreactive; Al is protected by a thin oxide layer.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 60,"o": "Boron is unreactive in crystalline form. Aluminium forms a very thin oxide layer on the surface which protects the metal from further attack."},{"sec": "Group 13: oxidation states and chemical properties","ids": [861,862],"t": "Amorphous B and Al burn to B2O3, Al2O3; with N2 at high T give nitrides. 4E + 3O2 → 2E2O3; 2E + N2 → 2EN","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 60,"o": "Amorphous boron and aluminium metal on heating in air form B2O3 and Al2O3 respectively. With dinitrogen at high temperature they form nitrides. 4E(s) + 3O2(g) → 2E2O3(s); 2E(s) + N2(g) → 2EN(s)"},{"sec": "Group 13: oxidation states and chemical properties","ids": [862,863,864],"t": "**B2O3 acidic (forms borates); Al2O3 and Ga2O3 amphoteric; In2O3 and Tl2O3 basic.**","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 60,"o": "The nature of these oxides varies down the group. Boron trioxide is acidic and reacts with basic (metallic) oxides forming metal borates. Aluminium and gallium oxides are amphoteric and those of indium and thallium are basic in their properties."},{"sec": "Group 13: oxidation states and chemical properties","ids": [865],"t": "B is unaffected by acids and alkalies at moderate T; **Al dissolves in both (amphoteric)**.","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 60,"o": "Boron does not react with acids and alkalies even at moderate temperature; but aluminium dissolves in mineral acids and aqueous alkalies and thus shows amphoteric character."},{"sec": "Group 13: oxidation states and chemical properties","ids": [866],"t": "2Al(s) + 6HCl(aq) → 2Al^3+(aq) + 6Cl^-(aq) + 3H2(g)","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 60,"o": "Aluminium dissolves in dilute HCl and liberates dihydrogen. 2Al(s) + 6HCl(aq) → 2Al^3+(aq) + 6Cl^-(aq) + 3H2(g)"},{"sec": "Group 13: oxidation states and chemical properties","ids": [866],"t": "**Concentrated HNO3 makes Al passive** (protective oxide).","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 60,"o": "However, concentrated nitric acid renders aluminium passive by forming a protective oxide layer on the surface."},{"sec": "Group 13: oxidation states and chemical properties","ids": [867],"t": "2Al + 2NaOH + 6H2O → 2Na^+[Al(OH)4]^- (sodium tetrahydroxoaluminate(III)) + 3H2","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 60,"o": "Aluminium also reacts with aqueous alkali and liberates dihydrogen. 2Al(s) + 2NaOH(aq) + 6H2O(l) → 2Na^+[Al(OH)4]^-(aq) (sodium tetrahydroxoaluminate(III)) + 3H2(g)"},{"sec": "Group 13: oxidation states and chemical properties","ids": [867],"t": "All form trihalides, **except TlI3**. 2E + 3X2 → 2EX3","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 60,"o": "These elements react with halogens to form trihalides (except TlI3). 2E(s) + 3X2(g) → 2EX3(s) (X = F, Cl, Br, I)"},{"sec": "Group 13: oxidation states and chemical properties","ids": [870,871],"t": "Anhydrous AlCl3 fumes in moist air: partial hydrolysis releases HCl (white fumes).","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 61,"o": "Anhydrous aluminium chloride is partially hydrolysed with atmospheric moisture to liberate HCl gas. Moist HCl appears white in colour."},{"sec": "Anomalous properties of boron","ids": [872,873],"t": "Trichlorides, tribromides, triiodides are covalent and hydrolyse; [M(OH)4]^- and [M(H2O)6]^3+ exist in water **for all except B**.","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 61,"o": "The tri-chlorides, bromides and iodides of all these elements being covalent in nature are hydrolysed in water. Species like tetrahedral [M(OH)4]^- and octahedral [M(H2O)6]^3+, except in boron, exist in aqueous medium."},{"sec": "Anomalous properties of boron","ids": [874,875,876],"t": "Monomeric trihalides are strong Lewis acids: F3B + :NH3 → F3B←NH3","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 61,"o": "The monomeric trihalides, being electron deficient, are strong Lewis acids. Boron trifluoride easily reacts with Lewis bases such as NH3 to complete octet around boron. F3B + :NH3 → F3B←NH3"},{"sec": "Anomalous properties of boron","ids": [876,877],"t": "**B: max covalence 4 (no d orbitals)**; Al and heavier can exceed 4.","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 61,"o": "It is due to the absence of d orbitals that the maximum covalence of B is 4. Since the d orbitals are available with Al and other elements, the maximum covalence can be expected beyond 4."},{"sec": "Anomalous properties of boron","ids": [878,879],"t": "Other trihalides (e.g. AlCl3) dimerise by halogen bridging (Al2Cl6), completing the metal's octet.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 61,"o": "Most of the other metal halides (e.g., AlCl3) are dimerised through halogen bridging (e.g., Al2Cl6). The metal species completes its octet by accepting electrons from halogen in these halogen bridged molecules."},{"sec": "Anomalous properties of boron","ids": [880,882,883],"t": "**BF6^3- does not exist**: B can't expand its octet.","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 61,"o": "Boron is unable to form BF6^3- ion. Due to non-availability of d orbitals, boron is unable to expand its octet. Therefore, the maximum covalence of boron cannot exceed 4."},{"sec": "Borax","ids": [885,886,887],"t": "Borax Na2B4O7·10H2O: white crystalline; actually contains [B4O5(OH)4]^2-, so correct formula **Na2[B4O5(OH)4]·8H2O**.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 61,"o": "Borax is the most important compound of boron. It is a white crystalline solid of formula Na2B4O7·10H2O. In fact it contains the tetranuclear units [B4O5(OH)4]^2- and correct formula; therefore, is Na2[B4O5(OH)4]·8H2O."},{"sec": "Borax","ids": [888,889],"t": "Aqueous borax is alkaline: Na2B4O7 + 7H2O → 2NaOH + 4H3BO3","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 61,"o": "Borax dissolves in water to give an alkaline solution. Na2B4O7 + 7H2O → 2NaOH + 4H3BO3 (orthoboric acid)"},{"sec": "Borax","ids": [889,890],"t": "Heating: borax loses water and swells, melts to a transparent liquid, solidifies to a glassy borax bead.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 61,"o": "On heating, borax first loses water molecules and swells up. On further heating it turns into a transparent liquid, which solidifies into glass like material known as borax bead."},{"sec": "Borax","ids": [891],"t": "Na2B4O7·10H2O → Na2B4O7 → 2NaBO2 (sodium metaborate) + B2O3 (boric anhydride)","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 61,"o": "Na2B4O7·10H2O → Na2B4O7 → 2NaBO2 (sodium metaborate) + B2O3 (boric anhydride) (heat)"},{"sec": "Borax","ids": [891],"t": "Transition-metal metaborates are coloured, hence the borax bead test.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 61,"o": "The metaborates of many transition metals have characteristic colours and, therefore, borax bead test can be used to identify them in the laboratory."},{"sec": "Borax","ids": [892],"t": "Borax + CoO in the flame: **blue Co(BO2)2 bead**.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 61,"o": "For example, when borax is heated in a Bunsen burner flame with CoO on a loop of platinum wire, a blue coloured Co(BO2)2 bead is formed."},{"sec": "Orthoboric acid","ids": [892,893],"t": "H3BO3: white crystalline, soapy; sparingly soluble in cold water, very soluble in hot.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 61,"o": "Orthoboric acid, H3BO3 is a white crystalline solid, with soapy touch. It is sparingly soluble in water but highly soluble in hot water."},{"sec": "Orthoboric acid","ids": [894,895],"t": "From acidified borax: Na2B4O7 + 2HCl + 5H2O → 2NaCl + 4B(OH)3","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 61,"o": "It can be prepared by acidifying an aqueous solution of borax. Na2B4O7 + 2HCl + 5H2O → 2NaCl + 4B(OH)3"},{"sec": "Orthoboric acid","ids": [895],"t": "Also formed by hydrolysis of most boron compounds (halides, hydrides).","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 61,"o": "It is also formed by the hydrolysis (reaction with water or dilute acid) of most boron compounds (halides, hydrides, etc.)."},{"sec": "Orthoboric acid","ids": [896,897],"t": "Layer structure: planar BO3 units joined by H-bonds.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 61,"o": "It has a layer structure in which planar BO3 units are joined by hydrogen bonds."},{"sec": "Orthoboric acid","ids": [898,899],"t": "**Boric acid: weak monobasic Lewis acid, not a protonic acid.** B(OH)3 + 2HOH → [B(OH)4]^- + H3O^+","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 62,"o": "Boric acid is a weak monobasic acid. It is not a protonic acid but acts as a Lewis acid by accepting electrons from a hydroxyl ion: B(OH)3 + 2HOH → [B(OH)4]^- + H3O^+"},{"sec": "Orthoboric acid","ids": [899,900],"t": "Heating: H3BO3 → HBO2 (metaboric acid, above 370 K) → B2O3","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 62,"o": "On heating, orthoboric acid above 370K forms metaboric acid, HBO2 which on further heating yields boric oxide, B2O3. H3BO3 → HBO2 → B2O3 (heat)"},{"sec": "Orthoboric acid","ids": [902,903],"t": "It can't release H^+ itself; it accepts OH^- from water to complete its octet, releasing H^+.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 62,"o": "Because it is not able to release H^+ ions on its own. It receives OH^- ions from water molecule to complete its octet and in turn releases H^+ ions."},{"sec": "Diborane","ids": [904,905],"t": "B2H6 (simplest borane): 4BF3 + 3LiAlH4 → 2B2H6 + 3LiF + 3AlF3 (diethyl ether)","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 62,"o": "The simplest boron hydride known, is diborane. It is prepared by treating boron trifluoride with LiAlH4 in diethyl ether. 4BF3 + 3LiAlH4 → 2B2H6 + 3LiF + 3AlF3"},{"sec": "Diborane","ids": [905],"t": "Lab: 2NaBH4 + I2 → B2H6 + 2NaI + H2","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 62,"o": "A convenient laboratory method for the preparation of diborane involves the oxidation of sodium borohydride with iodine. 2NaBH4 + I2 → B2H6 + 2NaI + H2"},{"sec": "Diborane","ids": [905,906],"t": "Industrial: 2BF3 + 6NaH → B2H6 + 6NaF (450 K)","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 62,"o": "Diborane is produced on an industrial scale by the reaction of BF3 with sodium hydride. 2BF3 + 6NaH → B2H6 + 6NaF (450 K)"},{"sec": "Diborane","ids": [906],"t": "B2H6: colourless, highly toxic, b.p. 180 K; catches fire spontaneously in air.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 62,"o": "Diborane is a colourless, highly toxic gas with a b.p. of 180 K. Diborane catches fire spontaneously upon exposure to air."},{"sec": "Diborane","ids": [907,908],"t": "B2H6 + 3O2 → B2O3 + 3H2O; ∆cH° = -1976 kJ mol^-1","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 62,"o": "It burns in oxygen releasing an enormous amount of energy. B2H6 + 3O2 → B2O3 + 3H2O; ∆cH° = -1976 kJ mol^-1"},{"sec": "Diborane","ids": [909,910,911],"t": "Higher boranes are also spontaneously flammable. B2H6(g) + 6H2O(l) → 2B(OH)3(aq) + 6H2(g)","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 62,"o": "Most of the higher boranes are also spontaneously flammable in air. Boranes are readily hydrolysed by water to give boric acid. B2H6(g) + 6H2O(l) → 2B(OH)3(aq) + 6H2(g)"},{"sec": "Diborane","ids": [911],"t": "Cleaved by Lewis bases to BH3·L: B2H6 + 2NMe3 → 2BH3·NMe3; B2H6 + 2CO → 2BH3·CO","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 62,"o": "Diborane undergoes cleavage reactions with Lewis bases (L) to give borane adducts, BH3·L. B2H6 + 2NMe3 → 2BH3·NMe3; B2H6 + 2CO → 2BH3·CO"},{"sec": "Diborane","ids": [911],"t": "With NH3: B2H6·2NH3 = [BH2(NH3)2]^+[BH4]^-; further heating gives **borazine B3N3H6 (\"inorganic benzene\")**.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 62,"o": "Reaction of ammonia with diborane gives initially B2H6·2NH3 which is formulated as [BH2(NH3)2]^+[BH4]^-; further heating gives borazine, B3N3H6 known as \"inorganic benzene\"."},{"sec": "Diborane","ids": [912,913],"t": "Structure: 4 terminal H and 2 B in one plane; 2 bridging H above and below.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 62,"o": "The four terminal hydrogen atoms and the two boron atoms lie in one plane. Above and below this plane, there are two bridging hydrogen atoms."},{"sec": "Diborane","ids": [914,915],"t": "4 terminal B–H are 2c–2e bonds; 2 bridging B–H–B are **3c–2e bonds**.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 62,"o": "The four terminal B-H bonds are regular two centre-two electron bonds while the two bridge (B-H-B) bonds are different and can be described in terms of three centre-two electron bonds."},{"sec": "Diborane","ids": [916,917],"t": "Tetrahedral [BH4]^- (hydridoborates).","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 63,"o": "Boron also forms a series of hydridoborates; the most important one is the tetrahedral [BH4]^- ion. Tetrahydridoborates of several metals are known."},{"sec": "Diborane","ids": [918],"t": "2MH + B2H6 → 2M^+[BH4]^- (M = Li, Na; diethyl ether)","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 63,"o": "Lithium and sodium tetrahydridoborates, also known as borohydrides, are prepared by the reaction of metal hydrides with B2H6 in diethyl ether. 2MH + B2H6 → 2M^+[BH4]^- (M = Li or Na)"},{"sec": "Diborane","ids": [919,920],"t": "Each B uses sp^3 hybrids; one of the four is empty.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 63,"o": "Each B atom uses sp^3 hybrids for bonding. Out of the four sp^3 hybrids on each B atom, one is without an electron."},{"sec": "Diborane","ids": [921,922],"t": "Bridge bonds (3c–2e) are called **banana bonds**.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 63,"o": "The terminal B-H bonds are normal 2-centre-2-electron bonds but the two bridge bonds are 3-centre-2-electron bonds. The 3-centre-2-electron bridge bonds are also referred to as banana bonds."},{"sec": "Diborane","ids": [923,924],"t": "LiBH4, NaBH4: reducing agents in organic synthesis; used to make other borohydrides.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 63,"o": "Both LiBH4 and NaBH4 are used as reducing agents in organic synthesis. They are useful starting materials for preparing other metal borohydrides."},{"sec": "Uses of boron and aluminium","ids": [925,926],"t": "B: hard, refractory, high m.p., low density, very low conductivity; B fibres in bullet-proof vests and light aircraft composites.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 63,"o": "Boron being extremely hard refractory solid of high melting point, low density and very low electrical conductivity, finds many applications. Boron fibres are used in making bullet-proof vest and light composite material for aircraft."},{"sec": "Uses of boron and aluminium","ids": [927],"t": "^10B absorbs neutrons strongly: metal borides as shields and control rods.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 63,"o": "The boron-10 (^10B) isotope has high ability to absorb neutrons and, therefore, metal borides are used in nuclear industry as protective shields and control rods."},{"sec": "Uses of boron and aluminium","ids": [928],"t": "Borax/boric acid: heat-resistant glass (Pyrex), glass-wool, fibreglass.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 63,"o": "The main industrial application of borax and boric acid is in the manufacture of heat resistant glasses (e.g., Pyrex), glass-wool and fibreglass."},{"sec": "Uses of boron and aluminium","ids": [929,930],"t": "Borax: soldering flux, glazes for earthenware, medicinal soaps; aqueous boric acid: mild antiseptic.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 63,"o": "Borax is also used as a flux for soldering metals, for heat, scratch and stain resistant glazed coating to earthenwares and as constituent of medicinal soaps. An aqueous solution of orthoboric acid is generally used as a mild antiseptic."},{"sec": "Uses of boron and aluminium","ids": [931,932,933],"t": "Al: bright silvery-white, high tensile strength, good conductor; **weight for weight, twice the electrical conductivity of Cu**.","exc": true,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 63,"o": "Aluminium is a bright silvery-white metal, with high tensile strength. It has a high electrical and thermal conductivity. On a weight-to-weight basis, the electrical conductivity of aluminium is twice that of copper."},{"sec": "Uses of boron and aluminium","ids": [935],"t": "Al alloys with Cu, Mn, Mg, Si, Zn; used in packing, utensils, construction, aircraft, transport.","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 63,"o": "It forms alloys with Cu, Mn, Mg, Si and Zn. Aluminium and its alloys can be given shapes of pipe, tubes, rods, wires, plates or foils and, therefore, find uses in packing, utensil making, construction, aeroplane and transportation industry."},{"sec": "Uses of boron and aluminium","ids": [936],"t": "Domestic use of Al is now reduced (toxicity).","exc": false,"tab": false,"src": "old11","page": 63,"o": "The use of aluminium and its compounds for domestic purposes is now reduced considerably because of their toxic nature."}]);
})();
